<%# The "Example" cell's definition-site link, shared by the three panels that print an example's
    own coordinate: "Unannotated examples", "Slowest tests" and "Examples in this spec file".

    ONE partial rather than three copies. The three carried this block byte-identical — the residue
    of SPGD-695, which propagated one convention into three panels by hand. The cost of that is
    already on the page: when "Unstable test runs" later needed the same cell it hand-wrote a fourth
    variant. A convention copied is a convention that drifts, and this one has a specific way of
    drifting badly — the class string, the `rel`, and above all WHICH of the two branches carries
    the link are decisions the panels must not answer differently.

    A PARTIAL AND NOT A HELPER, deliberately. `RepositoriesHelper` states the rule this would break:
    its readers "return a plain String and not `html_safe` markup, so escaping is ERB's". Markup
    belongs in a view; the helper file has no `link_to`/`tag.`/`safe_join` in it and this change
    does not introduce the first one.

    `observation` is the only REQUIRED local, and that is load-bearing rather than terse
    (`link_name:` below is optional and defaults off). `@repository` and `@latest_test_run` are read
    as ivars — they are already in scope at all three call sites, both loaded long before any of
    these partials render. The rule is about not passing a `repository:` local, which would invite a
    caller to re-resolve it per row — exactly what the two `count_queries` budget examples ("costs
    the same number of queries for a hundred linked rows as for three", and its ten-row sibling)
    exist to catch. An optional presentational flag costs nothing per row and does not breach that.
    A partial render itself issues no queries, and `#github_blob_url` is string composition that
    asks GitHub nothing, so the per-row cost of this cell stays zero.

    WHICH LINE CARRIES THE LINK is the branch below. `SpecObservation#label` falls back to the
    definition site for a nameless row, so on that branch the coordinate IS the row's whole
    identity and wears the link alone — rendering the location line underneath as well would make
    it wear the same text twice. A named row shows the name on the first line and the coordinate
    beneath it. THE COORDINATE ALWAYS CARRIES A LINK, on both branches. On a named row the name
    ALSO carries one when a caller passes `link_name:` — only "Slowest tests" does, and that cell
    then holds two links to two different places. Absent the flag the named row prints its name
    plainly, exactly as it did before the flag existed.

    `#location_label` is `file_path` + `line_number` — the DEFINITION site, never `spec_file_path`,
    which for a shared example group would pair two halves from different files.

    Pinned against `@latest_test_run.commit_sha`: the sha of the run the page is anchored to, so the
    link lands on the code as it was when it ran. That is the shared rule here and NOT a universal —
    "Unstable test runs" is run-by-run and pins each row to its own `row.commit_sha`, which is why
    it is not a caller of this partial.

    NEW TAB: the reader is mid-worklist, and losing a list narrowed by two asks to follow one link
    would make the panel worse to use the more of it you work through. `rel` is written out rather
    than left to the browsers that imply it. %>
<%# `link_name:` — OFF unless a caller asks for it, hence `local_assigns` rather than a required
    local. It turns the named branch's first line into a link into "This test, run by run"
    (`_unstable_test_runs.html.erb`), through `drill_down_path` so opening a test closes no file,
    area, description, branch or run the reader opened separately, with `aria-current` on the row
    whose test is open — the conventions `_unstable_tests.html.erb` sets for that destination, and
    its reasons carry here unchanged. Only "Slowest tests" opts in: it ranks wall clocks against
    ONE run, so its reader's first question about a row — always this slow, or slow today — is
    answered there and by nothing on this page. That is a CALLER's claim about its own reader
    rather than a decision this shared cell makes for all three, and the caller states its side.

    THE LABEL IS LINKED AND THE NAME IS ASKED. `#label` falls back to the definition site for a
    nameless row — a coordinate the destination cannot resolve — so the ask is keyed on `name`.
    Inside this branch `name.present?` is already true and the two read alike; keying on `name` is
    what keeps that true rather than leaving it to hold by coincidence. No new tab and no `rel`,
    unlike the coordinate below it: the destination is another panel of this same page. %>
<% link_name = local_assigns.fetch(:link_name, false) %>
<% definition_site_url = @repository.github_blob_url(observation.file_path,
                                                     observation.line_number,
                                                     @latest_test_run.commit_sha) %>
<% if observation.name.present? %>
  <% if link_name %>
    <%= link_to observation.label,
                drill_down_path(@repository, unstable_test: observation.name,
                                anchor: "unstable-test-runs"),
                class: "underline underline-offset-2 decoration-app-border-light " \
                       "text-app-content hover:decoration-app-content",
                aria: { current: observation.name == @unstable_test_request ? "true" : nil } %>
  <% else %>
    <%= observation.label %>
  <% end %>
  <span class="block font-mono text-xs text-app-muted">
    <%= link_to observation.location_label, definition_site_url,
                class: "underline underline-offset-2 decoration-app-border-light " \
                       "text-app-content hover:decoration-app-content",
                target: "_blank", rel: "noopener noreferrer" %>
  </span>
<% else %>
  <%= link_to observation.label, definition_site_url,
              class: "underline underline-offset-2 decoration-app-border-light " \
                     "text-app-content hover:decoration-app-content",
              target: "_blank", rel: "noopener noreferrer" %>
<% end %>
